// logos.lcm.canon.metaclass — the metaclass base types (ADR 0021).
//
// A `container Map<K, V> {...}` item is a DECLARATION — not a type. The
// container's value type (the handle a program works with) is whatever the
// factory machinery produces for a (store, configuration) pair; it is not
// spelled by the declaration. The bridge from the declaration level to the
// type level is `typeof`:
//
//     typeof(Map::<i64, i64>)  ==  CtrClass<@{ "kind": "ordered_map",
//                                                    "key": <type:i64>,
//                                                    "value": <type:i64> }>
//
// `CtrClass<CFG>` is nothing deeper than a TYPED WRAPPER over the Writ
// configuration constant — the tag that says how to process the document
// (route it to the container factory). Identity comes from the document's
// content hash; the wrapper supplies dispatch. Instantiating the wrapper is
// what the mono→factory seam (ADR 0021 §3) intercepts: Canon judges the
// config, the factory emits the family. A future second metaclass gets its
// own wrapper over the same WritStatic mechanics.
package logos.lcm.canon.metaclass;

use logos.lang.writ.wstatic;
use logos.lang.rc;                // Rc — the thread-local container handle plane
use logos.mem.sync;               // Arc — the snapshot parent-hold plane
use logos.mem.bt.map;             // trait Snapshot — the erased public snapshot tier
// The factory-generated VECTOR family delegates into the btvec positional
// prototype, and a metaprog-emitted `use` cannot itself LOAD a module — the
// same hub rule as logos.mem.bt.map's node/btss edges. Every create_ctr
// consumer imports THIS module, so the edge lives here (map itself cannot
// carry it: map → btvec → memstore → map would cycle).
use logos.mem.bt.btvec;
// Same hub rule for the batch machinery (BtssBuf / btss_batch_insert) the
// generated map families delegate into.
use logos.mem.bt.pack;

/// Typed wrapper over a container configuration document. Zero-field: the
/// type carries only the CFG identity (content hash) + factory routing.
pub struct CtrClass<const CFG: WritStatic> {}

/// The container-family protocol (ADR 0021 Phase 4a). The snapshot arrives
/// ERASED as `Arc<dyn Snapshot>` — the public tier. Erasure unifies every
/// store behind one public snapshot type, so the family binds per CFG alone
/// (no `<S>` parameter): a second store presents the SAME `dyn Snapshot` and
/// its generated container recovers its own concrete internally. The metaclass
/// factory closes each drained demand by emitting
///
///     impl CtrFamily for CtrClass<CFG> {
///         type Handle = Hs<hex>;
///         fn family_id() -> u64 { ... }
///         fn create(snap, ctr_id) -> Hs<hex> { return Hs<hex>::create(snap, ctr_id); }
///         fn open(snap, ctr_id)   -> Hs<hex> { return Hs<hex>::open(snap, ctr_id); }
///     }
///
/// with the demand's CFG document spliced verbatim as the const type-arg —
/// re-parsing it yields the same content hash, hence the very CtrClass
/// instance mono demanded. `family_id()` returns that hash; `Handle` is the
/// generated CoW handle (a thin `{snap, ctr_id}` view). All fns are SELF-LESS
/// (the trait grammar has no `static fn`). The impl is also the drain loop's
/// satisfaction signal (driver-side probe, main.cpp).
pub trait CtrFamily {
    type Handle;
    fn family_id() -> u64;
    /// The snapshot arrives ERASED as `&Arc<dyn Snapshot>`: the handle CLONES
    /// the Arc — a container holds its PARENT snapshot alive (the ownership
    /// chain Ctr ──Arc→ Snapshot ──Arc→ Store).
    fn create(snap: &Arc<dyn Snapshot>, ctr_id: u64) -> Self::Handle;
    fn open(snap: &Arc<dyn Snapshot>, ctr_id: u64) -> Self::Handle;
}

/// The LEAF-BATCH face of a container family (ADR 0025 §5, S1): the two generated
/// types a hand-written consumer otherwise has NO WAY TO NAME.
///
/// A family's types carry the config content hash (`Hs352959f3caf5b795LeafWalk`), and
/// that bare spelling resolves in EMITTED code only — the chunk imports the producer's
/// defining package through `native_use_text`. A test, an oracle, or any hand-written
/// caller has no such door: measured, the bare hash name in a user program's type
/// annotation is `unknown type '…LeafWalk'`, and `use logos.gen;` is
/// `cannot find package`. The projection below is that door, exactly as
/// `CtrFamily::Handle` is the door to the handle:
///
///     pub type LedWalk  = <typeof(Led) as CtrLeafFamily>::LeafWalk;
///     pub type LedBatch = <typeof(Led) as CtrLeafFamily>::LeafBatch;
///     fn first(s: &mut LedWalk) -> LedBatch { … }
///
/// WHY A SECOND TRAIT AND NOT TWO MORE MEMBERS ON `CtrFamily` — MEASURED, and this is
/// the whole reason this trait exists. `impl CtrFamily` is emitted for ordered_map,
/// multimap AND vector; `{N}LeafBatch`/`{N}LeafWalk` are emitted by the ordered_map arm
/// ALONE. With the two members on `CtrFamily`, a `kind vector` family stops compiling
/// (`error [impl CtrFamily for CtrClass$…]: unknown type 'Hs…LeafBatch'`) — and the
/// stdlib build does NOT catch it, because no stdlib unit instantiates a vector family
/// through the factory. Splitting the capability into its own trait is also what §3
/// says to do with a capability in the first place: EXISTENCE = TRAIT MEMBERSHIP. A
/// family that produces leaf batches implements this; one that does not, does not, and
/// the projection is refused at the call site instead of breaking the family's own
/// emission. Pinned in BOTH directions (`pass/ctr_leaf_family_spelling` admits for
/// ordered_map and `pass/ctr_vec_leaf_family_spelling` for vector,
/// `fail/ctr_leaf_family_volume_refused` refuses for the VOLUME arm — S1b gave
/// the vector arm a producer, so the refusal moved to a family that still has
/// none rather than being dropped).
///
/// NO BOUND IS DECLARED ON `LeafWalk` (`type LeafWalk: BatchStream<Self::LeafBatch>`
/// would state §1's protocol at the trait, and it does compile). It is left off ON
/// PURPOSE: the bound needs `use logos.lang.stream;` HERE, and every `create_ctr`
/// consumer imports this module — which would hub `BatchStream::next` into all of them
/// and make `it.next()` ambiguous against `Iterator::next` for every iterator in the
/// tree. That is the precise hazard `logos.lang.stream`'s own header records as the
/// reason it is not in the prelude, and it is not worth re-opening for a restatement:
/// the emitted `impl BatchStream<{N}LeafBatch> for {N}LeafWalk` is the fact, and the
/// capability query (`has_trait`) is how the planner asks for it.
pub trait CtrLeafFamily: CtrFamily {
    /// One leaf's row window — the columnar batch of ADR §2, as the family emits it.
    type LeafBatch;
    /// The stream of those windows: one descent per LEAF, not a method per row (§5).
    type LeafWalk;
}

/// The single container-instantiation surface (ADR 0021 §4):
///
///     let m = create_ctr::<typeof(Map::<u64, u64>)>(&mut snap, ctr_id);
///
/// `typeof` over a container declaration yields its `CtrClass<CFG>`
/// wrapper; naming it here fires the mono demand, the factory generates the
/// family for `snap`'s store, and the handle type is inferred from the
/// per-store impl. Naming the handle type explicitly (a struct field, a
/// signature) is the qualified projection
/// `<typeof(...) as CtrFamily>::Handle` — deliberately
/// verbose until store profiles land (ADR 0020 §9).
///
/// NB (ADR 0021 §5): logos.lcm.deem's ADR-0017 `create_ctr`/`open_ctr` still
/// exist; these are package-scoped here — a unit importing BOTH modules would
/// collide (no unit does today; the deem pair retires with the memoria port).
pub fn create_ctr<C: CtrFamily>(snap: &Arc<dyn Snapshot>, ctr_id: u64) -> C::Handle {
    return C::create(snap, ctr_id);
}

/// Open an existing container `ctr_id` in `snap` through its family handle.
/// Existence is not checked here (wave-parity with the generated handle's
/// `open`); the Option-returning form arrives with the deem-pair retirement.
pub fn open_ctr<C: CtrFamily>(snap: &Arc<dyn Snapshot>, ctr_id: u64) -> C::Handle {
    return C::open(snap, ctr_id);
}

// ── the Rc plane (S3 Store-dyn design) ──────────────────────────────────────
// A container handle is STRICTLY THREAD-LOCAL — it rides Rc (non-atomic),
// while Store/Snapshot ride Arc (cross-thread). v1 allocates plainly; the
// design destination is a pool of ready handles inside the snapshot (Drop →
// pool — open question), which these signatures already permit.

/// Creates the container and hands the handle out on the thread-local Rc plane.
pub fn create_ctr_rc<C: CtrFamily>(snap: &Arc<dyn Snapshot>, ctr_id: u64) -> Rc<C::Handle> {
    return rc_new::<C::Handle>(C::create(snap, ctr_id));
}

/// Opens an existing container on the thread-local Rc plane.
pub fn open_ctr_rc<C: CtrFamily>(snap: &Arc<dyn Snapshot>, ctr_id: u64) -> Rc<C::Handle> {
    return rc_new::<C::Handle>(C::open(snap, ctr_id));
}
